{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":6,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1539066947,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDEzODI4MzA4NQ=="},"target":"ea531a2c36ac0a9218de077b2f0b33934ec3d613d1a522c0e5ba0f87d1fc5ed4","message":"Variants of this issue have been reported previously #1874 #2015 (private reports). Here is a demo that demos a particular aspect of this issue. Script to create 500 straight subdatasets of a single superdataset:\n\n```sh\nset -e -u\n\ndatalad create many\ncd many\nfor i in $(seq 500); do\n\techo $i\n\t/usr/bin/time -o ../create_timing.txt --append -f \"%D\\t%K\\t%M\\t%e\\t%S\\t%U\" datalad create -d . $i\ndone\n```\n\nPlotting the runtime log of each invocation of `create` we can clearly see a trend were the duration more than doubles from the first to the last run:\n\n![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/136479/46650727-f07fa000-cb9d-11e8-9eed-7efe8cb0576a.png)\n\nA likely source is the particular behavior of path annotation (which is already being addressed in https://github.com/datalad/datalad-revolution), but other explanations are possible.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"819663238374c863923453c36b916c251f565216"},"timestamp":1539075026,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQyODExMjcyNQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2908#issuecomment-428112725"},"message":"For comparison I did the same thing using just git/annex to see to what extend it's our responsibility:\n\n```sh\nset -e -u\n\nmkdir many\ncd many\ngit init\ngit annex init\nfor i in $(seq 500); do\n\techo $i\n\t/usr/bin/time -o ../create_timing.txt --append -f \"%D\\t%K\\t%M\\t%e\\t%S\\t%U\" /tmp/fake_create.sh $i\ndone\n```\n\n`fake_create`:\n\n```sh\nmkdir $1\ngit -C $1 init\ngit -C $1 annex init\ngit submodule add \"./$1\" $1\ngit commit -m \"Added $1\"\n```","files":null},{"type":6,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1539075026,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDEzODMyNzE2MQ=="},"target":"5644ca2f9bb63b7c8a87c68043164708c19268b646228fde744bc2ac29f7fdbd","message":"For comparison I did the same thing using just git/annex to see to what extend it's our responsibility:\n\n```sh\nset -e -u\n\nmkdir many\ncd many\ngit init\ngit annex init\nfor i in $(seq 500); do\n\techo $i\n\t/usr/bin/time -o ../create_timing.txt --append -f \"%D\\t%K\\t%M\\t%e\\t%S\\t%U\" /tmp/fake_create.sh $i\ndone\n```\n\n`fake_create`:\n\n```sh\nmkdir $1\ngit -C $1 init\ngit -C $1 annex init\ngit submodule add \"./$1\" $1\ngit commit -m \"Added $1\"\n```\n\n![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/136479/46657674-1f534180-cbb1-11e8-9941-43726f63e018.png)","files":null},{"type":6,"author":{"id":"819663238374c863923453c36b916c251f565216"},"timestamp":1539075223,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDEzODMyODIxNQ=="},"target":"5644ca2f9bb63b7c8a87c68043164708c19268b646228fde744bc2ac29f7fdbd","message":"For comparison I did the same thing using just git/annex to see to what extend it's our responsibility:\n\n```sh\nset -e -u\n\nmkdir many\ncd many\ngit init\ngit annex init\nfor i in $(seq 500); do\n\techo $i\n\t/usr/bin/time -o ../create_timing.txt --append -f \"%D\\t%K\\t%M\\t%e\\t%S\\t%U\" /tmp/fake_create.sh $i\ndone\n```\n\n`fake_create`:\n\n```sh\nmkdir $1\ngit -C $1 init\ngit -C $1 annex init\ngit submodule add \"./$1\" $1\ngit commit -m \"Added $1\"\n```\n\n![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/136479/46657674-1f534180-cbb1-11e8-9941-43726f63e018.png)\n\nSo, we do indeed make it worse. Not only by an offset.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"819663238374c863923453c36b916c251f565216"},"timestamp":1539075876,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQyODExNzIwNQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2908#issuecomment-428117205"},"message":"Apart from path annotation, we do query subdatasets from within `create` to figure a possible collision. That might contribute as well. But I agree - path annotation looks like the most important factor to me.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1539108697,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQyODI5NDA5Mg==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2908#issuecomment-428294092"},"message":"Update with various performance improvements from https://github.com/datalad/datalad-revolution/pull/12. Essentially there is only a single path annotation run (and a leaner one per create call). Also subdataset detection is now done differently.\n\n![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/136479/46687647-a6c5a280-cbfb-11e8-90c5-5a49b06f9142.png)\n\nHere is the same code, but with subdataset collision detection in the parentds disabled:\n\n![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/136479/46688562-f9a05980-cbfd-11e8-8ad3-c7d8cf73cb52.png)\n\nGoing one step further and completely disable any path annotation does not seem to have a major effect either.\n\nSide note:  the comparison with the plain git calls is not really appropriate, as those submodules do not even have a commit. We have machinery running that adds content to each subdataset etc.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1539153022,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQyODQ0Mzk3Mw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2908#issuecomment-428443973"},"message":"Another comparison, this time all in a single python session to avoid cost of repeated initialisation\n\n```py\nfrom datalad.api import rev_create as create\nfrom time import time\n\nds = create('many')\nfor i in range(400):\n    print(i)\n    t = time()\n    ds.rev_create('sub{}'.format(i))\n    with open('create_timing_py.txt', 'a') as f:\n        f.write('{}\\n'.format(time() - t))\n```\n\nThis is using `create` with all but the initial (single-)path annotation removed. For comparision I also disabled collision detection, and also the dry-run to figure out file sizes to be added. Nothing removes the trend. And lastly, switching to v6 also doesn't have an impact.\n\n![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/136479/46715182-3b152100-cc5f-11e8-8245-5213c73ef0d5.png)","files":null},{"type":6,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1539153022,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDEzODc1NTEyNQ=="},"target":"c90c54a1a0f67f876b1094d719bc52f66f5401cfae9e9b2ed21decce22f01dc4","message":"Another comparison, this time all in a single python session to avoid cost of repeated initialisation\n\nAll variations of this basic script:\n\n```py\nfrom datalad.api import rev_create as create\nfrom time import time\n\nds = create('many')\nfor i in range(200):\n    print(i)\n    t = time()\n    ds.rev_create('sub{}'.format(i))\n    with open('create_timing_py.txt', 'a') as f:\n        f.write('{}\\n'.format(time() - t))\n```\n\nThe following figure shows the comparison to current master performance (and as a sanity check the timing of just creating this many datasets in a subdir of another dataset without registering, also on master). The rest are various alternatives of the code in -revolution (with most, and lastly any path annotation removed, incl the parent collision detection).\n\n![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/136479/46716817-cabdce00-cc65-11e8-8333-b9df6e2e3595.png)\n\n\nConclusion: the performance improvements in -revolution are noticeable. However, the slowing is always present, and path annotation, not GitPython (compare, repeated DB init in commandline test above) are to blame, neither is the parent collision detection, as such.\n\nGiven that -revolution and -core do very different things for this test, the common element are `Repo.add()` and `Repo.add_submodule()`. However, it is not the dryrun call in `add()` either, that just adds a time constant.\n\nNothing removes the trend. And lastly, switching to v6 also has a constant offset.\n\nNot sure if we can do anything better at this point.","files":null},{"type":6,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1539153184,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDEzODc1NTg3Ng=="},"target":"c90c54a1a0f67f876b1094d719bc52f66f5401cfae9e9b2ed21decce22f01dc4","message":"Another comparison, this time all in a single python session to avoid cost of repeated initialisation\n\nAll variations of this basic script:\n\n```py\nfrom datalad.api import rev_create as create\nfrom time import time\n\nds = create('many')\nfor i in range(200):\n    print(i)\n    t = time()\n    ds.rev_create('sub{}'.format(i))\n    with open('create_timing_py.txt', 'a') as f:\n        f.write('{}\\n'.format(time() - t))\n```\n\nThe following figure shows the comparison to current master performance (and as a sanity check the timing of just creating this many datasets in a subdir of another dataset without registering, also on master). The rest are various alternatives of the code in -revolution (with most, and lastly any path annotation removed, incl the parent collision detection).\n\n![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/136479/46716817-cabdce00-cc65-11e8-8333-b9df6e2e3595.png)\n\n\nConclusion: the performance improvements in -revolution are noticeable. However, the slowing is always present, and neither path annotation, nor GitPython (compare, repeated DB init in commandline test above) are to blame, neither is the parent collision detection, as such.\n\nGiven that -revolution and -core do very different things for this test, the common element are `Repo.add()` and `Repo.add_submodule()`. However, it is not the dryrun call in `add()` either, that just adds a time constant.\n\nNothing removes the trend. And lastly, switching to v6 also has a constant offset.\n\nNot sure if we can do anything better at this point.","files":null},{"type":6,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1539153206,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDEzODc1NTk2OA=="},"target":"c90c54a1a0f67f876b1094d719bc52f66f5401cfae9e9b2ed21decce22f01dc4","message":"Another comparison, this time all in a single python session to avoid cost of repeated initialisation\n\nAll variations of this basic script:\n\n```py\nfrom datalad.api import rev_create as create\nfrom time import time\n\nds = create('many')\nfor i in range(200):\n    print(i)\n    t = time()\n    ds.rev_create('sub{}'.format(i))\n    with open('create_timing_py.txt', 'a') as f:\n        f.write('{}\\n'.format(time() - t))\n```\n\nThe following figure shows the comparison to current master performance (and as a sanity check the timing of just creating this many datasets in a subdir of another dataset without registering, also on master). The rest are various alternatives of the code in -revolution (with most, and lastly any path annotation removed, incl the parent collision detection).\n\n![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/136479/46716817-cabdce00-cc65-11e8-8333-b9df6e2e3595.png)\n\n\nConclusion: the performance improvements in -revolution are noticeable. However, the slowing is always present, and neither path annotation, nor GitPython (compare, repeated DB init in commandline test above) are to blame, neither is the parent collision detection, as such.\n\nGiven that -revolution and -core do very different things for this test, the common elements are `Repo.add()` and `Repo.add_submodule()`. However, it is not the dryrun call in `add()` either, that just adds a time constant.\n\nNothing removes the trend. And lastly, switching to v6 also has a constant offset.\n\nNot sure if we can do anything better at this point.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"819663238374c863923453c36b916c251f565216"},"timestamp":1539163754,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQyODUwMzQ1Nw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2908#issuecomment-428503457"},"message":"Improvement by revolution seems pretty good to me!\n\n\u003e Side note: the comparison with the plain git calls is not really appropriate, as those submodules do not even have a commit. We have machinery running that adds content to each subdataset etc.\n\nAlso tried to have a commit per submodule before adding as a submodule - didn't notably change anything. My point was to figure a lower boundary on what we possibly can achieve. Even that oversimplified plain git/annex simulation shows an increase by 30%. So, we can't remove the trend itself. We only can try to come close to this.\n\n\u003e Not sure if we can do anything better at this point.\n\nNot sure either. However, revolution seems to be the right approach. Since it aims to reduce the sheer number of git calls altogether, I think it's likely to yield additional improvement over time, even if just by a constant (which matters in reality).","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1539177491,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQyODU2NzY5NA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2908#issuecomment-428567694"},"message":"Alrighty. No need to keep this open any longer, I guess.","files":null},{"type":4,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1539177491,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDExOkNsb3NlZEV2ZW50MTg5NTQ4MjI3OA=="},"status":2}]}